{"title": "Introduction", "url": "https://doi.org/10.5422/fordham/9781531504717.003.0001", "provider": "crossref", "query_lane": "jesus_christ", "query": "grace dynamical systems theology", "retrieved_at": "2026-08-25T11:17:52.540755+00:00", "authors": ["Julia Feder"], "year": 2023, "abstract": "This chapter introduces the reader to the argument that healing from sexual trauma is a mystical-political practice that is both incarnational and eschatological. It is “incarnational” in that it is fully enfleshed and human. It is embodied, it is contextual, conditioned by history and circumstance, and—by virtue of God’s full embrace of human life and circumstance in the person of Jesus Christ—pregnant with God. It is “eschatological” in that it is partial and incomplete, requiring assistance from God and insufficient on its own terms, yet not desperately or depressingly so. In the Christian tradition, the incomplete and failing state of things is not the final word. And because the Christian story is a story of the persistence of relationships in the face of violence, this tradition contains rich resources to animate the mystical-political shape of posttraumatic healing.", "venue": "Incarnating Grace", "doi": "10.5422/fordham/9781531504717.003.0001", "cited_by_count": 0, "open_access_url": "", "provider_id": "10.5422/fordham/9781531504717.003.0001", "source_type": "scholarly_metadata", "math_signals": ["logic"], "theology_signals": ["christ", "god", "incarnation", "jesus", "sin"], "lane": "THEOLOGICAL_BRIDGE", "relevance_score": 60, "evidence_grade": "B", "screening_status": "SCREEN", "content_hash": "b0017e4f7178340b3cc722783514e46ab35b1d6e9d25f5100158575dc931a39f"}
{"title": "Waiting on Grace", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780198842194.001.0001", "provider": "crossref", "query_lane": "jesus_christ", "query": "grace dynamical systems theology", "retrieved_at": "2026-08-25T11:17:52.540755+00:00", "authors": ["Michael Barnes, SJ"], "year": 2020, "abstract": "Whereas much theology of religions regards ‘the other’ as a problem to be solved, this book begins with a Church called to witness to its faith in a multicultural world by practising a generous yet risky hospitality. A theology of dialogue takes its rise from the Christian experience of being-in-dialogue. Taking its rise from the biblical narrative of encounter, call, and response, such a theology cannot be fully understood without reference to the matrix of faith that Christians share in complex ways with the Jewish people. The contemporary experience of the Shoah, the dominating religious event of the twentieth century, has complexified that relationship and left an indelible mark on the religious sensibility of both Jews and Christians. Engaging with a range of thinkers, from Heschel, Levinas, and Edith Stein who were all deeply affected by the Shoah, to Metz, Panikkar, and Rowan Williams, who are always pressing the limits of what can and cannot be said with integrity about the self-revealing Word of God, this book shows how Judaism is a necessary, if not sufficient, source of Christian self-understanding. What is commended by this foundational engagement is a hope-filled ‘waiting on grace’ made possible by virtues of empathy and patience. A theology of dialogue focuses not on metaphysical abstractions but on biblical forms of thought about God’s presence to human beings which Christians share with Jews and, under the continuing guidance of the Spirit of Christ, learn to adapt to a whole range of contested cultural and political contexts.", "venue": "", "doi": "10.1093/oso/9780198842194.001.0001", "cited_by_count": 2, "open_access_url": "", "provider_id": "10.1093/oso/9780198842194.001.0001", "source_type": "scholarly_metadata", "math_signals": [], "theology_signals": ["biblical", "christ", "god", "grace", "sin", "theology"], "lane": "CHALLENGE", "relevance_score": 58, "evidence_grade": "B", "screening_status": "BACKGROUND_ONLY", "content_hash": "31398eb6b3dbb655c7f4fa7c71a926cec21795de0c71e8fcd9cd8919c3b89eab"}
{"title": "Conclusion", "url": "https://doi.org/10.5422/fordham/9781531504717.003.0010", "provider": "crossref", "query_lane": "jesus_christ", "query": "grace dynamical systems theology", "retrieved_at": "2026-08-25T11:17:52.540755+00:00", "authors": ["Julia Feder"], "year": 2023, "abstract": "Human beings wounded by sexual trauma need a deeply incarnational kind of posttraumatic healing. Sexual violence harms all of us who live within cultures of rape. Posttraumatic salvation must involve the healing of human beings in all their dimensions. Salvation cannot be limited to the restoration of an individual’s personal psychological health (or even simply a restoration of her personal relationship with God). Salvation in a posttraumatic context must extend to a restoration of interpersonal relationships and a transformation of rape culture. In this way, salvation is wholly bodily—it involves the healing of individual bodies, interpersonal and social bodies, and political bodies.", "venue": "Incarnating Grace", "doi": "10.5422/fordham/9781531504717.003.0010", "cited_by_count": 0, "open_access_url": "", "provider_id": "10.5422/fordham/9781531504717.003.0010", "source_type": "scholarly_metadata", "math_signals": ["logic"], "theology_signals": ["god", "incarnation", "salvation"], "lane": "THEOLOGICAL_BRIDGE", "relevance_score": 48, "evidence_grade": "C", "screening_status": "SCREEN", "content_hash": "2464f9cb40b238178cff1eff52e949bec4d945a8cc295a2d7a2070f2a2fa03ec"}
{"title": "Jesus of History, Christ of Faith: One Christ Jesus", "url": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.2946625", "provider": "crossref", "query_lane": "jesus_christ", "query": "Jesus Christ mathematical model", "retrieved_at": "2026-08-25T11:17:46.518636+00:00", "authors": ["Michael Quinlan"], "year": 2017, "abstract": "Whilst stressing the importance of grace and faith, the Catholic bible emphasizes the desirability of wisdom , reason, the need for the faithful to be able to explain their faith and the fact that the existence and some characteristics of God are ascertainable by reason. The real key to the New Testament is its emphasis on truth and on Jesus being the truth. Christianity asserts not only that faith in Christ is reasonable and rational but also that events in the life of Christ actually occurred as a matter of human history . As a result, the events described in the New Testament can be the subject of investigation by the application of historical method , archeology , psychology , medicine and other academic disciplines. The Catholic Church, also places great emphasis on reason and truth and has a long history of engagement considering and benefitting from a relationship between philosophy (an attempt by rational and reasoned consideration to answer questions of life’s meaning including the identification of principles underlying knowledge and being ) and theology (the exploration of the content and implications of divine revelation ). This paper seeks to explain the Catholic view of the relationship between philosophy and theology and to demonstrate their integration with each other. To do so the paper examines why philosophy and theology work, how they relate to each other in Catholic thought and how that relationship can be reconciled with the Magisterium. It concludes with a consideration of the present need for a relationship between philosophy and theology. It argues that the Jesus of History and the Christ of the Catholic faith are one and the same.", "venue": "", "doi": "10.2139/ssrn.2946625", "cited_by_count": 0, "open_access_url": "", "provider_id": "10.2139/ssrn.2946625", "source_type": "scholarly_metadata", "math_signals": [], "theology_signals": ["bible", "christ", "divine", "god", "grace", "jesus", "sin", "theology"], "lane": "BACKGROUND", "relevance_score": 48, "evidence_grade": "C", "screening_status": "BACKGROUND_ONLY", "content_hash": "605913f60da700477353de698c9c24348f6e6854b66bb14ce92e69841535b19d"}
